  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1216 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe extended forecast package will feature the continuation of \nwarmer and drier conditions through the weekend, before we see a \nsynoptic pattern change early next week. Where we could see the \nreturn of cooler and wetter conditions across the region. For the \nfirst half of the extended, a weak upper ridge will continue over \nthe Caprock regions, which will allow above seasonal normal \ntemperatures to continue in the 80s and 90s, along with dry \nconditions. However by the end of the weekend the synoptic pattern \nwill begin to change as an upper trough moves onshore the PacNW and \ndeepens over the western CONUS by Monday. In turn, the upper ridge \nwill shift east while becoming more amplified over the region. As we \nsee the return of southwest flow, moist subtropical moisture will \nwork to make its way back into the region. There is quite a bit of \nuncertainty with this part of the extended, especially with what is \nnow currently Tropical Depression Twenty-E. Depending on how this \nevolves, we could see more moisture transport than what is currently \nbeing depicted by NWP guidance. Given the large amount of \nuncertainty, we will go ahead and maintain NBM mentionable PoPs \nbeginning late Monday considering both deterministic and ensemble \nguidance are beginning to align with a wetter outcome. However, \nthere is still some disagreement with the GFS being the more bullish \nmodels compared to the ECMWF, which looks to be in part to the \nlocation of the upper trough which will play a part in how southerly \nflow aloft becomes.",
